                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "borrowing",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9c1161dc-8356-332d-b203-5d481473be02",
                                        "entry_id": "5e1f0566-9c6f-347f-aea5-ef4b54056459",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An instance of something being borrowed."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ebfc3b33-7dab-48e1-b65f-3d7f0ff35fa3",
                                                "sense_id": "9c1161dc-8356-332d-b203-5d481473be02",
                                                "text": "January 1834, Horace Binney, Speech on the Question of the Removal of the Deposites\nSubscriptions, borrowings of money, taxings of the citizens and their property, may all be valid, as operations by virtue of laws for the government of the City […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "92b21aa5-f321-3d2f-9f08-a77a447c7f4b",
                                        "entry_id": "5e1f0566-9c6f-347f-aea5-ef4b54056459",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A borrowed word, adopted from a foreign language; loanword."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0128f135-e878-4d06-b4ed-c5539841dbe7",
                                                "sense_id": "92b21aa5-f321-3d2f-9f08-a77a447c7f4b",
                                                "text": "Whilst you would be forgiven for thinking this statement comes straight from the latest UKIP manifesto, it is in fact a quotation from The Anglish Moot, a fan-page promoting the use of the 'Anglish' language — that is, English with all foreign borrowings stripped away."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "9c9ed7c2-058f-4b69-839a-b618a813dd7e",
                        "source_entry_id": "b5d2a651-8154-3bcb-96a4-185fd96b90c2",
                        "source_sense_id": "7fd4644a-920e-3214-9263-115d894bfe7c",
                        "target_lang_word": "loanword",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "loanword",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9f41e963-aadf-388d-a506-cd53b62335f1",
                                        "entry_id": "0bf9b539-7572-32b4-a79f-0fa39ec552db",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A word directly taken into one language from another one."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "38353b39-6f6d-4cf3-a46d-2fa14d3b515e",
                                                "sense_id": "9f41e963-aadf-388d-a506-cd53b62335f1",
                                                "text": "\"Calque\" is a loanword with French origins, and \"loanword\" is a calque with German origins."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "85db7944-e95e-4fa8-9f3e-0aaed3d3e5c3",
                                                "sense_id": "9f41e963-aadf-388d-a506-cd53b62335f1",
                                                "text": "New words, and particularly loanwords, are simplified, and hence naturalized in American much more quickly than in English. Employé has long since become employee in our newspapers, and asphalte has lost its final e, and manœuvre has become maneuver, and pyjamas has become pajamas."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3f10998a-36f6-422b-b304-f4e96a3e4a20",
                                                "sense_id": "9f41e963-aadf-388d-a506-cd53b62335f1",
                                                "text": "This searching was facilitated by the author's knowledge of the vagaries of Anglo-Indian spelling and the numerous colonial-era transliteration systems used for loanwords from Indian languages."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4ef88fc5-708d-4626-bebe-81918f68eb28",
                                                "sense_id": "9f41e963-aadf-388d-a506-cd53b62335f1",
                                                "text": "Indeed, in 1957 Xenophon Zolotas, the then governor of the Bank of Greece, gave two speeches to the IMF that contained just Greek loanwords apart from the inevitable basic English. (Example: “Our policies should be based more on economic and less on political criteria.”)"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
